<h2>What Is a House In Multiple Occupation?</h2>
<p>A House In Multiple Occupation is a property rented by at least three people who are not from one household but share facilities such as a kitchen or bathroom. Houses In Multiple Occupation are a cornerstone of affordable housing in UK cities, particularly Birmingham, where demand for flexible, cost-effective accommodation continues to grow.</p>

<h2>When Is a Licence Required?</h2>
<p>Under the Housing Act 2004, mandatory House In Multiple Occupation licensing applies to any property occupied by five or more people from two or more separate households. However, Birmingham City Council operates an Additional Licensing scheme that extends this requirement to smaller Houses In Multiple Occupation with three or four occupants in certain wards.</p>
<p>Operating an unlicensed House In Multiple Occupation is a criminal offence. Penalties can include an unlimited fine, a Rent Repayment Order forcing you to repay up to 12 months of rent, and inclusion on the national rogue landlord database — consequences that can permanently damage your reputation as a landlord.</p>

