<h2>Key Standards Your Property Must Meet</h2>
<p>To obtain and maintain a House In Multiple Occupation licence, your property must satisfy a range of physical and management standards. Bedroom sizes must meet minimum floor area requirements — at least 6.51m² for a single adult. You'll also need working fire detection systems, adequate bathroom and kitchen facilities scaled to the number of occupants, a valid Gas Safety Certificate renewed annually, an Electrical Installation Condition Report (EICR) valid for five years, and a completed fire risk assessment.</p>

<h2>The Licence Application Process</h2>
<p>Applying for a House In Multiple Occupation licence involves submitting a detailed application to your local authority, providing evidence that you and any managing agents are fit and proper persons, and paying the relevant fee. Licences are typically granted for up to five years, after which renewal is required. Local councils may also carry out inspections to verify compliance before issuing or renewing a licence.</p>

